        "output": "To solve this problem, we first need to establish the ratio of half-gallons of milk to dozen cookies. Since Juan needs 10 half-gallons of milk to bake 40 cookies, which is equivalent to 3 dozen cookies (1 dozen = 12 cookies), we can calculate the amount of milk needed per dozen cookies.\n\nWe set up a proportion: \n\\[ \\frac{10\\text{ half-gallons}}{40\\text{ cookies}} = \\frac{x}{3\\text{ dozen cookies}} \\]\n\nSolving for \\(x\\) gives us:\n\\[ x = \\frac{10 \\times 3}{40} = \\frac{30}{40} = \\frac{3}{4}\\text{ half-gallons per dozen cookies} \\]\n\nNow, we need to find out how many half-gallons of milk Juan needs for 200 dozen cookies. We multiply the amount needed per dozen by the total number of dozens:\n\\[ \\frac{3}{4}\\text{ half-gallons per dozen} \\times 200\\text{ dozen cookies} = 150\\text{ half-gallons} \\]\n\nTherefore, Juan needs 150 half-gallons of milk to bake 200 dozen cookies.",
